WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.040
A four-time Oscar nominee, a favorite&nbsp;
of director Stanley Kubrick, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:12.101
multiple mobsters. Let's take a closer look into&nbsp;
the lives of the stars we've lost so far in 2022.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.120
Iconic singer and actor Meat Loaf, who won a&nbsp;
Grammy for his hit ballad "I'd Do Anything For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.000
Love (But I Won't Do That)" and appeared in films&nbsp;
like "The Rocky Horror Picture Show" and "Fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.520
Club," died in January 2022. His manager&nbsp;
confirmed the news to The New York Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.680
but did not immediately reveal&nbsp;
the cause of death. He was 74.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.960
Born Marvin Lee Aday, the Dallas native&nbsp;
adopted his childhood nickname as a stage name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.760
early in his career. He got his big break when&nbsp;
he auditioned for the off-Broadway musical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.800
"More Than You Deserve" in the early '70s. He&nbsp;
won a role and formed a lifelong partnership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.520
with writer Jim Steinman, who would go on to pen&nbsp;
Meat Loaf's debut album, 1977's "Bat Out of Hell."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.720
Todd Rundgren produced the record, and everyone&nbsp;
from the New York Philharmonic to members of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.320
E Street Band contributed. It was an unlikely&nbsp;
hit that critics begrudgingly praised, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.360
Meat Loaf would struggle to replicate its success&nbsp;
in the years that followed. After a few flops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:15.840
he dropped 1993's "Bat Out of Hell II: Back Into&nbsp;
Hell," which included his Grammy-winning single.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.760
In terms of films, Meat Loaf is best&nbsp;
known for playing Eddie the delivery boy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.400
in 1975's "The Rocky Horror Picture&nbsp;
Show" and former bodybuilder Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.240
Paulson in 1999's "Fight Club." Other notable&nbsp;
credits include 1992's "Wayne's World,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:37.868
2001's "The 51st State," and "Ghost Wars,"&nbsp;
on which he recurred between 2017 and 2018.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.880
Comedian and actor Bob Saget, best known&nbsp;
for his long stint on the sitcom "Full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:49.040
House," died in January 2022 in his hotel room.&nbsp;
There was no evidence of drug use or foul play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.840
according to Chief Medical Examiner&nbsp;
Joshua Stephany. He was 65. His wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.880
Kelly Rizzo Saget, said in&nbsp;
a statement, per Variety,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.240
"I am so completely shattered and in disbelief."

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.920
Saget made his TV debut as Bob the Comic&nbsp;
in a 1981 episode of the Tom Hanks sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.400
"Bosom Buddies." In 1987, he won the&nbsp;
part of Danny Tanner on "Full House,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:17.760
which became a career-defining role. Saget played&nbsp;
the widowed father of three from 1987 to 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.760
and reprised the role in the Netflix-produced&nbsp;
sequel series, "Fuller House."

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.720
"Well just remember, when children seem the&nbsp;
least lovable, means they need love the most."

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.720
It was often hectic on set, but Saget&nbsp;
had fond memories of working with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.600
onscreen kids. The actor told Vanity Fair in 2014,

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.920
"Jodie Sweetin was four when she started,&nbsp;
Ashley and Mary-Kate were nine months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.080
Candace was nine — you're talking about&nbsp;
exceptionally talented young kids."
 &nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.880
Elsewhere, Saget was known as the voice of&nbsp;
the older Ted Mosby on "How I Met Your Mother"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.143
and as the host of "America's Funniest Home&nbsp;
Videos," which he fronted for almost a decade.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.200
French actor Gaspard Ulliel was best known&nbsp;
to international audiences for his turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.240
as the young Hannibal Lecter. Sadly, he&nbsp;
died due to a skiing accident in January&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.080
2022, as reported by Deadline. He was 37.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.240
Ulliel began his career on French&nbsp;
television in the late 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.440
and was landing film roles by the early&nbsp;
2000s. His big break arrived in 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.320
when he landed the part of Manech in "A&nbsp;
Very Long Engagement." Ulliel won his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.880
first Cesar award for this movie and would&nbsp;
make the transition to Hollywood soon after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.560
playing the titular killer in the 2007 prequel&nbsp;
film "Hannibal Rising." It was one of his biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:38.320
roles at the time of his death. But the actor was&nbsp;
also about to make his debut as a Marvel villain.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:44.160
Sadly, Ulliel died a few months before Marvel's&nbsp;
"Moon Knight" dropped. He plays Anton Mogart, aka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.875
Midnight Man, a black market antiquities dealer&nbsp;
who goes head to head with the titular hero.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.840
Emmy winner Louie Anderson, who appeared&nbsp;
in films like "Ferris Bueller's Day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:59.040
Off" and "Coming to America," died of&nbsp;
complications from cancer in January 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.800
his publicist confirmed to CNN. He was 68.&nbsp;
Tributes from across the entertainment world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.400
poured in after the news broke. Screenwriter&nbsp;
Carl Kurlander wrote in a Deadline tribute piece,

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.160
"I was one of the lucky ones who got&nbsp;
to call Louie a friend. He made me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.640
and others in his orbit feel like family."

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.200
Anderson decided to give stand-up a go after&nbsp;
his colleagues dared him to go on stage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.320
The audience loved him, and it wasn't long before&nbsp;
he was making his mark in the comedy world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.474
Anderson's talent and career were multifaceted.

00:04:29.474 --> 00:04:33.440
He was as comfortable in front of a crowd&nbsp;
as he was fronting game shows. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.920
he would become a fixture on American TV screens&nbsp;
as the host of "Family Feud" from 1999 to 2002.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.720
Anderson plied his trade on shows like "Grace&nbsp;
Under Fire," "Touched by an Angel," "Chicago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.200
Hope," and "Scrubs," but some of his best&nbsp;
work came in the twilight of his career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.960
Anderson was nominated for Emmys three&nbsp;
years running for his performance on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.120
Zach Galifianakis-led FX comedy&nbsp;
"Baskets," winning in 2016.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.680
"Oh, presents! Unnecessary."

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:09.600
Hollywood legend Sidney Poitier, widely seen as&nbsp;
the first Black movie star, died in January 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.200
at the age of 94, as confirmed by The New&nbsp;
York Times. Barack Obama said in a tweet,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.120
"Through his groundbreaking roles&nbsp;
and singular talent, Sidney Poitier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.400
epitomized dignity and grace, revealing the&nbsp;
power of movies to bring us closer together."

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.440
Denzel Washington said of him&nbsp;
in an interview with People,

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.320
"He was a gentle man and opened doors for&nbsp;
all of us that had been closed for years."

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.240
Poitier earned his first credited film role,&nbsp;
Dr. Luther Brooks, in 1950's "No Way Out."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.360
He actively avoided demeaning roles, and by the&nbsp;
end of the decade, his persistence began paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.680
off. He became the first Black man to be nominated&nbsp;
for the Academy Award for Best Actor in 1959,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.080
blowing critics away with his turn as an&nbsp;
escaped prisoner in "The Defiant Ones."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.240
He missed out on that occasion, but would&nbsp;
make history a few years later when he became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.160
the first Black man to win that award for his&nbsp;
performance as an traveling laborer in 1963's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.520
"Lilies of the Field." How did&nbsp;
Poitier feel about opening so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.000
many doors for Black actors and artists&nbsp;
everywhere? He once told Oprah Winfrey,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.160
"It's been an enormous responsibility.&nbsp;
And I accepted it, and I lived in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.234
way that showed how I respected&nbsp;
that responsibility. I had to."

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:25.920
Up-and-coming actor Moses J. Moseley was&nbsp;
found dead in Georgia in January 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:29.280
His agent, Tabatha Minchew,&nbsp;
told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.080
"Moses was a very talented person, with a&nbsp;
bright light around him. He will be missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:40.000
deeply by his friends, family and fans.&nbsp;
Always a ball of happy energy around him."

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.400
The cause of death was not immediately revealed&nbsp;
by authorities, who launched an investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.360
to determine the circumstances. He&nbsp;
was 31. A native of South Carolina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:54.080
Moseley studied at Georgia State&nbsp;
University before settling down in Atlanta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.480
According to The Hollywood Reporter, he worked&nbsp;
as a model before transitioning into acting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:03.040
Moseley's first credited role was playing&nbsp;
Michonne's pet walker Mike on "The Walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:09.200
Dead" in 2012. He would appear on the hit AMC&nbsp;
show half a dozen times over the next few years.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.280
Moseley went on to play Tic Toc on&nbsp;
USA Network's "Queen of the South,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:18.320
an usher on HBO's critically-acclaimed&nbsp;
"Watchmen" series, and Kadeem on Irv Gotti's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.680
"Tales." His most notable feature film&nbsp;
was 2017's "Attack of the Southern Fried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.560
Zombies," in which he played Robbie. Moseley&nbsp;
had several projects in various stages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.301
production when he was reported missing by his&nbsp;
family, including "Hank" and "Cadillac Respect."

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.600
Academy Award winner William Hurt — who owned&nbsp;
the '80s with hits like "The Big Chill" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:45.200
"Gorky Park" — died in March 2022. The&nbsp;
actor was 71 years old, and as his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.440
Will, said in a statement via Variety,

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.880
"He died peacefully, among&nbsp;
family, of natural causes."

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.200
Although not initially publicly confirmed, we&nbsp;
now know that Hurt died from complications of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:04.720
prostate cancer; he was first diagnosed in 2018.&nbsp;
Born in Washington, D.C., Hurt studied theology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.160
at Tufts before finding his passion in acting.&nbsp;
The actor made his debut film performance with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:15.520
"Altered States," a psychedelic trip that mixes&nbsp;
mushrooms, religious imagery, and body horror.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.880
He followed that up with the sexy noir flick "Body&nbsp;
Heat" — the film that propelled him to stardom.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:26.560
After that, Hurt won a Best Actor Oscar for&nbsp;
his portrayal of gay prisoner Luis Molina in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:31.520
"Kiss of the Spider Woman." It was just the&nbsp;
first of four times he'd be up for an Oscar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.440
with the Academy nominating his work for&nbsp;
"Children of a Lesser God," "Broadcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.960
News," and "A History of Violence." Fans will also&nbsp;
recognize Hurt from projects like "The Village"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.880
and "A.I. Artificial Intelligence." Of course,&nbsp;
the man also left his stamp on the superhero genre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.935
by playing Thaddeus Ross, the MCU antagonist&nbsp;
who pursued Bruce Banner and Natasha Romanoff.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.560
Estelle Harris came to fame as&nbsp;
an actor well into adulthood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.040
most familiar to TV audiences as&nbsp;
Estelle Costanza, the combative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:06.640
shrill-voiced mother of George Costanza on&nbsp;
the mega-popular '90s sitcom "Seinfeld."

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:09.360
"Oh, I can't believe it. Frank, come here!."

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.640
Harris appeared in nearly 30 episodes of&nbsp;
the series. She also used that distinctive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.040
angry voice in a number of other projects&nbsp;
post-"Seinfeld," including the "Toy Story"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.520
movies, as Mrs. Potato Head, "Brother&nbsp;
Bear," "Teacher's Pet," and "Futurama."

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:28.800
But Harris was much more than an argumentative,&nbsp;
uniquely voiced maternal figure. She had more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.080
than 100 acting credits to her name, all&nbsp;
of them coming in her late 40s and beyond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.000
Harris started her acting career in&nbsp;
community theater, regional theater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.720
and summer stock before segueing into&nbsp;
TV with appearances in commercials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:48.320
According to CNN, she once appeared in&nbsp;
25 nationally broadcast ads in one year.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.800
According to a statement from Harris'&nbsp;
son, Glen, via agent Michael Eisenstadt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:59.734
Estelle Harris died on April 2, 2022. A cause&nbsp;
of death was not provided; the actor was 93.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.520
Never a leading man and almost always a supporting&nbsp;
character in broad comedies and family movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.560
Gilbert Gottfried became a household name&nbsp;
because of his public and on-screen persona.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.280
It's hard not to forget that perpetually irate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:18.480
squinting malcontent screaming his thoughts in&nbsp;
a shrill, scratchy, overblown New York accent.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:23.200
"I can't believe it! I just don't believe it!"

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:28.560
After a major breakthrough as a member of the&nbsp;
cast of "Saturday Night Live" in the early 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.160
Gottfried became a fixture in '80s and '90s&nbsp;
movies, oft-rerun cultural touchstones for kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.760
and teens who grew up in that era. He played&nbsp;
Sidney Bernstein in "Beverly Hills Cop II,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.840
Mr. Peabody in all three "Problem&nbsp;
Child" movies, and voiced Iago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:49.280
in "Aladdin." He also voiced the Aflac duck in&nbsp;
a long-running series of insurance commercials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.000
That's to say nothing of his dozens of&nbsp;
other appearances in TV shows and movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.280
making use of his purposely annoying all-purpose&nbsp;
characterization. Gottfried was also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:04.880
relentlessly performing stand-up comedian and a&nbsp;
dark and twisted one favored by other knock-around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:10.000
comics, as evidenced by his appearances on&nbsp;
Comedy Central's series of celebrity roasts.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.200
On April 12, 2022, Gottfried's Twitter account&nbsp;
broke the news that the actor and comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:20.935
had died following an unspecified&nbsp;
"long illness." Gottfried was 67.

00:11:21.238 --> 00:11:26.320
Mike Hagerty was among the most prolific and&nbsp;
recognizable character actors of the last 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.760
years, popping up in more than 100 movies&nbsp;
and TV shows. He usually played a gruff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:36.880
blue-collar, working man type with a big&nbsp;
mustache and pronounced Chicagoan accent.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.920
Among Hagerty's most familiar roles, there's&nbsp;
Davey, a cable TV station employee in "Wayne's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:46.880
World" and apartment building superintendent&nbsp;
Mr. Treeger on "Friends," who becomes Joey's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:50.800
ballroom dance partner. He also played&nbsp;
best friend of the main character in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:56.000
original "Overboard," an auto shop owner&nbsp;
on "Lucky Louie," and a main cast role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.720
on "The George Carlin Show." Hagerty recently&nbsp;
played a captain on multiple episodes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:06.160
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine," appeared on "Shameless," and&nbsp;
played farmer Ed Miller on "Somebody Somewhere."

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:10.800
On her Instagram page, "Somebody Somewhere"&nbsp;
star Bridget Everett reported that Hagerty had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:16.880
died on May 5, 2022, in Los Angeles. A cause&nbsp;
of death was later revealed to be a seizure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.334
as a result of an adverse reaction&nbsp;
to antibiotics. He was 67 years old.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.080
One of the finest and most&nbsp;
recognizable actors of his era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:31.280
Ray Liotta alternated between rugged leading&nbsp;
man roles and quirky character actor parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.960
usually playing tough guys,&nbsp;
criminals, and intimidating figures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:40.480
as well as frequently sending up his public&nbsp;
image as a man of simmering, unhinged intensity.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:41.920
"Who you think we're talking to?"

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:48.400
Liotta was a prolific actor, appearing in more&nbsp;
than 100 movies and TV shows since the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:53.040
He broke out in a big way with back-to-back&nbsp;
roles as the ghost of Shoeless Joe Jackson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:58.880
in 1989's "Field of Dreams" and real-life&nbsp;
Mafia big shot Henry Hill in "Goodfellas,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:04.400
Martin Scorsese's classic crime epic that&nbsp;
would earn six Oscar nominations. That film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:09.840
solidified Liotta's specialty of playing men&nbsp;
just barely on one side of the law or the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:14.400
similar to his performances in "Something&nbsp;
Wild," "Cop Land," "Unlawful Entry,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:18.880
"Narc," "Observe and Report," "Shades of&nbsp;
Blues," and "The Many Saints of Newark."

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:23.520
According to Deadline, Liotta was in&nbsp;
the Dominican Republic in May 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:28.634
filming the movie "Dangerous Waters,"&nbsp;
when he died in his sleep. He was 67.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:34.800
In a career spanning more than 200 roles back&nbsp;
to the early 1970s, Philip Baker Hall became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:40.160
quintessential character actor. He's probably&nbsp;
best known to audiences as Lt. Joe Bookman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:45.600
a "library cop" tenaciously investigating&nbsp;
Jerry Seinfeld for a decades-overdue book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:50.320
on the comedian's titular show. You'll also&nbsp;
likely recognize him as the intimidating but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:54.960
secretly sweet neighbor Walt on "Modern&nbsp;
Family," Doctor Morrison on "Curb Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:00.000
Enthusiasm," and hippo game show host&nbsp;
Hank Hippopopalous on "BoJack Horseman."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.560
Writer-director Paul Thomas Anderson also&nbsp;
cast Hall in prominent roles in his first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:08.240
three features — "Hard Eight,"&nbsp;
"Boogie Nights," and "Magnolia."

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:10.880
According to Hall's daughter,&nbsp;
via The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:17.301
the actor died at home in Glendale, California,&nbsp;
on June 12, 2022. Hall was 90 years old.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:22.320
A chameleonic and frequently employed&nbsp;
character actor, Joe Turkel racked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:29.040
a whopping 142 credits over a career that&nbsp;
spanned from the late 1940s to the 1990s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:33.520
Of course, he's most closely associated with&nbsp;
the films of Stanley Kubrick, who cast the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:38.560
actor in three films. Turkel starred in "The&nbsp;
Killing" and "Paths of Glory," but he's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:44.080
famous for his appearance in "The Shining," where&nbsp;
he played the creepy ghost hotel bartender Lloyd.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:48.240
Turkel was virtually retired by the&nbsp;
1980s, when Ridley Scott hired him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:54.000
to play replicant maker Dr. Eldon Tyrell&nbsp;
in the 1982 sci-fi classic "Blade Runner."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:59.040
Other than those major film works, Turkel was&nbsp;
a constant presence over decades of television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:04.000
with one-shot roles on dozens of memorable&nbsp;
shows, including "Miami Vice," "Fantasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:09.120
Island," "Kojak," "The Andy Griffith&nbsp;
Show," "Bonanza," "Ironside," and "Combat!"

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:11.920
According to Variety, Turkel&nbsp;
died in a Santa Monica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:16.654
California, hospital on June 27, 2022. He was 94.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:21.520
One of the best and most definitive actors&nbsp;
of his generation, James Caan starred in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:25.680
some of the most highly regarded films&nbsp;
of the latter half of the 20th century.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.960
Usually playing tough guys,&nbsp;
criminals, intimidating dudes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:35.440
macho men, and inscrutable patriarchs, Caan paid&nbsp;
his dues on 1960s TV dramas like "Naked City,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:41.120
"The Untouchables," and "Wagon Train." Moving into&nbsp;
different roles in the early 1970s, Caan starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:45.440
as the title character in the adaptation&nbsp;
of John Updike's "Rabbit Run" and played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:50.800
real-life football player Brian Piccolo in the&nbsp;
acclaimed made-for-TV tearjerker "Brian's Song."

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:52.960
"Aye-yuh, sounds like a ball."

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:58.320
He found probably his most iconic role&nbsp;
ever as Sonny Corleone in "The Godfather,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:02.000
for which he received an Oscar&nbsp;
nomination. Later in his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:06.560
Caan would land memorable starring roles&nbsp;
in "Alien Nation," "Misery," "Honeymoon in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:13.760
Vegas," and "Elf," and join the frothy NBC&nbsp;
drama "Las Vegas." Sadly, on July 7, 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:19.000
Caan's family announced on Twitter that the&nbsp;
actor died the previous evening. He was 82.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:24.880
Tony Sirico found the role of his life as&nbsp;
Peter Paul Gualtieri, aka Paulie Walnuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:30.880
on HBO's universally acclaimed modern-day&nbsp;
Mafia saga "The Sopranos." An explosive, funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:37.280
and loyal lieutenant to mob boss Tony Soprano,&nbsp;
Sirico provided comic relief as Paulie Walnuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:41.360
offering up terse one-liners&nbsp;
and amusing mispronunciations.

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:43.520
"What do ya hear, what do you say?!"

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:49.760
He played similar characters in "Goodfellas,"&nbsp;
"Bullets Over Broadway," and "Mighty Aphrodite."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:53.360
In recent years, and after "The&nbsp;
Sopranos" ended, Sirico traded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:58.080
on his image and tendency to be typecast,&nbsp;
playing tough characters on comedy shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:04.000
like a gangster on "American Dad" and a gruff&nbsp;
dog on "Family Guy." Sirico's manager confirmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:09.830
to media outlets that the actor died&nbsp;
on Friday, July 8, 2022. He was 79.

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:14.720
Actor Dale Critchlow, who was best known for&nbsp;
playing Lyle the farmer in the offbeat cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:21.520
classic "Napoleon Dynamite," died in February&nbsp;
2022, as reported by Deadline. He was 92.

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:27.120
A native of Utah who moved to Idaho in his 30s,&nbsp;
Critchlow landed his small but memorable role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:32.000
by pure chance. The film was shot on&nbsp;
location in Idaho, where Critchlow lived and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:36.400
worked as a real farmer. He needed help with&nbsp;
his animals one day, so his wife reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:40.800
out to the mother of writer-director&nbsp;
Jared Hess. He told East Idaho News,

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:44.560
"My wife called Jared Hess' mother and&nbsp;
asked if one of her boys was there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:48.880
He came over, and he had a friend with him.&nbsp;
I drove the truck out into the pasture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.480
and the sheep came over. They grabbed&nbsp;
those bucks and pushed them into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:56.400
truck [...] and then later, he came back&nbsp;
and [said], 'I want you to be in my movie.'"

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:00.960
Critchlow became well known in his hometown&nbsp;
of Preston after the film became a success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:05.520
and was always happy to pose for pictures.&nbsp;
He went on to feature in another comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:11.040
2006's "Church Ball," which was considerably&nbsp;
less successful. But Critchlow remained famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:16.800
for his turn in "Napoleon Dynamite," despite not&nbsp;
being overly keen on the film. He said in 2020,

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:26.160
"I thought it was kind of funny in places, and&nbsp;
in a few places, it was kind of dragged out."

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.480
